=== L2 Metals and Alloys ===
id: a581a5cd-0fd4-45a8-8bfb-3271906089ef
slug: metals-and-alloys
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:44:38.396863+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 19

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Ferrous and non-ferrous metals, carbon content, alloying and how properties change through processing.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Ferrous and Non-Ferrous Metals</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">All metals used in engineering fall into one of two families. <dfn class="term" data-def="Metals that contain iron as their main element; most ferrous metals are magnetic and susceptible to rusting unless protected.">Ferrous metals</dfn> contain iron. This includes all the steels and cast iron. Because they contain iron, most ferrous metals are magnetic and will corrode in moist air if left unprotected &mdash; a thin layer of rust forms on the surface and, unlike the oxide layer on aluminium, it does not protect the metal underneath. <dfn class="term" data-def="Metals that do not contain iron as their main element; they are generally corrosion-resistant, non-magnetic and lighter than steel.">Non-ferrous metals</dfn> do not contain iron. Examples include aluminium, copper, lead and zinc. They are generally corrosion-resistant and non-magnetic, making them valuable in electrical, marine and food-contact applications.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and name three ferrous metals and three non-ferrous metals. For each, write one application where that metal is used.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Ferrous metals contain iron and are mostly magnetic and corrosion-prone: cast iron, low carbon steel, high carbon steel, stainless steel. Non-ferrous metals contain no iron and are mostly corrosion-resistant and non-magnetic: aluminium, copper, lead, zinc.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Steels and the Role of Carbon</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">Steel is iron with a controlled amount of carbon added. Increasing the carbon content raises hardness and strength but reduces ductility and toughness. <dfn class="term" data-def="Steel containing approximately 0.1% to 0.3% carbon; it is tough, ductile and readily welded, making it suitable for structural sections, car body panels and bolts.">Low carbon steel</dfn> (also called mild steel) contains around 0.1% to 0.3% carbon. It is tough, ductile and easy to form and weld, so it is the most widely used structural metal &mdash; think RSJ beams, car body panels and the bracket used to mount a gate post. Low carbon steel does not harden well through heat treatment, which means it cannot be used for cutting tools.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="Steel containing approximately 0.6% to 1.4% carbon; it is very hard and can be heat-treated to produce cutting edges, but it is less ductile than low carbon steel.">High carbon steel</dfn> contains approximately 0.6% to 1.4% carbon. The extra carbon makes it much harder and it responds well to heat treatment (hardening and quenching), so it is used for springs, chisels, files, drill bits and cutting tools. The trade-off is reduced ductility and weldability &mdash; high carbon steel can crack if welded without careful preheat. <dfn class="term" data-def="Steel alloyed with chromium (at least 10.5%) and often nickel, giving it excellent corrosion resistance and a shiny surface; used for kitchen equipment, medical instruments and chemical plant.">Stainless steel</dfn> is an alloy of iron, carbon and at least 10.5% chromium. The chromium forms a passive oxide layer that prevents corrosion, so stainless steel does not rust even in wet or acidic environments. It is used for surgical instruments, food-processing equipment and architectural cladding.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Cast Iron</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n7"><dfn class="term" data-def="An iron alloy with very high carbon content (2%&ndash;4%), making it hard but brittle; it casts well into complex shapes and damps vibration effectively.">Cast iron</dfn> contains 2% to 4% carbon &mdash; much more than any steel. This makes it very hard, but also brittle: it cannot be bent, drawn or welded without cracking. What it can do is pour beautifully into moulds, picking up fine surface detail, and it damps vibration extremely well. These properties make it ideal for machine beds (the heavy base of a lathe that must absorb machining vibrations), engine blocks and manhole covers. Cast iron has good compressive strength but poor tensile strength, so it must never be used in tension.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Non-Ferrous Metals in Engineering</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Aluminium is the most widely used non-ferrous metal in engineering. It has a density of around 2.7 g/cm<sup>3</sup> &mdash; roughly one third that of steel &mdash; giving an excellent strength-to-weight ratio. It is highly ductile, corrosion-resistant (a natural oxide layer forms on the surface) and a good electrical conductor. These properties make it the default choice for aircraft fuselage panels, vehicle wheels, drink cans and overhead power cables. Copper is an outstanding electrical and thermal conductor, highly ductile and corrosion-resistant. It is drawn into electrical wiring, shaped into heat-exchanger tubing and used in plumbing. Lead is very dense, soft, malleable and highly resistant to corrosion and radiation &mdash; used for X-ray shielding and roofing flashings. Zinc is used mainly for the <dfn class="term" data-def="A process where a thin layer of zinc is applied to steel to protect it from corrosion; the zinc acts as a sacrificial anode.">galvanising</dfn> of steel: a zinc coating corrodes preferentially, protecting the steel beneath.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="Without looking, explain in two sentences why aluminium is chosen over steel for aircraft structures, naming at least two properties.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Aluminium has roughly one-third the density of steel but retains good strength, corrosion resistance and ductility. This strength-to-weight ratio makes it essential wherever reducing mass is critical &mdash; aircraft, high-performance vehicles and bicycle frames.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Alloys and How They Change Properties</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">An <dfn class="term" data-def="A metallic material formed by combining two or more elements, at least one of which is a metal, to produce properties superior to those of the individual elements alone.">alloy</dfn> is formed by melting two or more elements together, usually to improve on the properties of the base metal alone. The additional atoms disrupt the regular crystal lattice of the metal, making it harder for dislocations (the defects that allow slip) to move &mdash; this is why alloys are almost always harder and stronger than the pure metals they are made from. Brass (copper + zinc) is harder than either copper or zinc alone, and easier to machine into precision components than pure copper. Bronze (copper + tin) is harder than copper and more resistant to wear, making it ideal for bearings, propellers and bushings. Brass is also corrosion-resistant and has a gold appearance, so it is used for plumbing fittings, musical instruments and decorative hardware.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Changing Properties: Cold Working, Grain Size and Heat Treatment</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Properties are not fixed at casting &mdash; they can be changed by working and heat treatment. <dfn class="term" data-def="Shaping a metal at room temperature by rolling, pressing or drawing, which increases its hardness and strength but reduces its ductility.">Cold working</dfn> (pressing, drawing or rolling at room temperature) distorts the crystal grains, introducing dislocations that tangle and block further movement. The result is a harder, stronger but less ductile metal. This is why cold-drawn steel rods are stronger than the equivalent hot-rolled material. If ductility needs to be restored, annealing (heating and slow cooling) recrystallises the grains and relieves the internal stresses.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14">For high carbon steel, hardening by <dfn class="term" data-def="Heating a high-carbon steel to above its critical temperature and then cooling it very rapidly (usually in water or oil) to trap a hard, brittle crystal structure called martensite.">quenching</dfn> involves heating the steel above its critical temperature (typically above 723°C) and then plunging it into water or oil. The rapid cooling traps a hard, brittle crystal structure called martensite. Tempering follows: the steel is reheated to a lower temperature (150°C to 600°C) to allow some internal stress relief and recovery of toughness, at the cost of a small reduction in hardness. Reducing grain size also increases strength: finer grains mean more grain boundaries to impede dislocation movement.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n16">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Ferrous metals contain iron (steels, cast iron) and are mostly magnetic and corrosion-prone; non-ferrous metals (aluminium, copper, brass, bronze) contain no iron and are generally corrosion-resistant and non-magnetic.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Increasing carbon content in steel raises hardness and strength but reduces ductility: low carbon steel is tough and weldable; high carbon steel can be hardened and quenched for cutting tools; cast iron (2&ndash;4% carbon) is hard and brittle with good vibration damping.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n19">Alloying, cold working, quenching and tempering all change the mechanical properties of metals &mdash; engineers use these processes deliberately to tune the balance between hardness, strength and toughness for a specific application.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n15">When asked to justify a metal choice for a component, name both the metal AND the specific property, then link it to the application &mdash; for example &ldquo;low carbon steel is suitable for the bracket because it is ductile and can be bent to shape without cracking, and it can be welded to the frame easily.&rdquo; Do not just write &ldquo;strong&rdquo; or &ldquo;cheap.&rdquo; If the question asks about carbon content and steels, be specific: low carbon (0.1&ndash;0.3%), high carbon (0.6&ndash;1.4%), cast iron (2&ndash;4%). These ranges are expected in extended-answer questions.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
