=== L3 Polymers and Composites ===
id: 8918ef6b-1ec8-48a7-a848-f4c20d2b9b6e
slug: polymers-and-composites
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-15T20:55:48.085291+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 18

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Thermoplastics, thermosets, fibre-reinforced polymers, structural timber, MDF and concrete explained.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Polymers: Two Fundamental Families</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Polymers are large molecules built from repeating chemical units called monomers. In engineering they divide cleanly into two families based on how they behave when heated. <dfn class="term" data-def="A polymer that softens and can be reshaped when heated, and returns to a solid when cooled; this process can be repeated many times.">Thermoplastics</dfn> soften on heating and harden again on cooling &mdash; the long molecular chains can slide past one another when warm. This means they can be remelted and reshaped, which makes them recyclable and suitable for injection moulding, vacuum forming and extrusion. <dfn class="term" data-def="A polymer that sets permanently when first heated and cannot be softened or reshaped by reheating; the molecular chains form permanent cross-links during curing.">Thermosetting polymers</dfn> (thermosets) form permanent cross-links between their molecular chains during curing. Once set, reheating does not soften them &mdash; it chars or burns them instead. This gives thermosets superior heat and chemical resistance, making them useful for electrical fittings, cookware handles and adhesives.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n3" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write down three thermoplastics and three thermosets from memory. Add one application beside each material.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Thermoplastics: ABS, acrylic, nylon, polycarbonate, polystyrene. Thermosets: epoxy resin, polyester resin, melamine, polyurethane, vulcanised rubber. A critical exam trap: polypropylene is NOT on this specification &mdash; only polystyrene is listed as the named foam/rigid thermoplastic.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">Named Thermoplastics and Their Uses</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n5">ABS (acrylonitrile butadiene styrene) is tough, rigid and easily moulded into complex shapes. It is used for computer keyboard keys, printer housings, crash helmets and LEGO bricks. Acrylic (<dfn class="term" data-def="A transparent thermoplastic also known as polymethylmethacrylate (PMMA), used where glass-like clarity is needed but lighter weight or impact resistance is preferable.">PMMA</dfn>) is transparent, UV-stable and weather-resistant, with a glass-like clarity at half the density of glass. It is used for shop display units, rear light lenses and aircraft windows. Nylon is self-lubricating, tough and wear-resistant: ideal for gear wheels, bearings and cable-tie heads that must operate without added grease. Polycarbonate combines high impact resistance with transparency &mdash; it is used for safety visors, CD/DVD discs and bullet-resistant glazing. Polystyrene is rigid and a good thermal insulator in its expanded (foam) form; in its high-impact form it is used for food packaging trays, model kits and disposable cutlery.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Named Thermosets and Their Uses</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n6">Epoxy resin is a two-part adhesive and matrix material: once mixed and cured, it is strong, rigid and resistant to chemicals and moisture. It bonds metals, ceramics and composites. Polyester resin is the most common matrix for glass-reinforced plastic (GRP) boat hulls and automotive body kits &mdash; cheaper than epoxy but slightly less strong. Melamine is hard, heat-resistant and hygienic: used for kitchen worktops and children&rsquo;s tableware. Polyurethane provides thermal insulation in rigid foam panels and is used as a coating resin. <dfn class="term" data-def="Natural rubber that has been treated with sulfur under heat to form permanent cross-links, giving it elasticity, toughness and resistance to wear and temperature.">Vulcanised rubber</dfn> is natural rubber with sulfur cross-links added: it is tough, elastic and resistant to wear &mdash; essential for vehicle tyres, seals and conveyor belts.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Composites: Matrix Plus Reinforcement</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">A <dfn class="term" data-def="An engineering material made from two or more constituent materials with different properties that, when combined, produce a material with characteristics superior to either component alone.">composite</dfn> consists of a reinforcing material embedded in a matrix. The matrix (usually a polymer resin) holds the fibres in position and transfers load; the reinforcement (fibres or particles) provides strength and stiffness. Neither component alone has the combined performance of the composite. The properties of a composite depend on: the type of reinforcement, the direction of the fibres, the volume fraction of fibres, and the type of matrix.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Glass-reinforced plastic (GRP) uses glass fibres in a polyester or epoxy matrix. It is lightweight, corrosion-resistant and relatively cheap: used for boat hulls, roof panels and sports car bodies. Carbon-fibre reinforced polymer (CFRP) uses carbon fibres in an epoxy matrix. It is stiffer and stronger than GRP for the same weight &mdash; the Boeing 787 uses a CFRP fuselage and F1 cars use CFRP monocoque chassis because the material combines very high stiffness with very low density. Fibre direction matters critically: fibres aligned along the load direction give maximum strength in that direction but are weak across it. Woven fabrics or cross-ply laminates are used when loads come from multiple directions.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="Close this and explain in two sentences why carbon fibre composites are used in aerospace, linking reinforcement direction to the way loads act in flight.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>In a composite, strength and stiffness are controlled by the reinforcement, but the fibres must be oriented along the direction of the applied load. Fibres running across the load direction contribute almost nothing to that load. This is why CFRP components are carefully designed as multi-directional laminates.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Structural Timber, Board Products and Ceramics</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Structural-grade timber is graded by strength and used where a lightweight, renewable structural material is needed. C16 and C24 are the most common grades (the number indicates characteristic bending strength in N/mm<sup>2</sup>). Timber is strong along the grain but weaker across it &mdash; this is why structural beams run lengthwise. <dfn class="term" data-def="An engineered wood panel made from thin layers of wood veneer glued together with the grain direction of alternate layers at right angles, giving uniform strength in both directions.">Plywood</dfn> is made from thin veneers glued with alternating grain directions. Cross-graining overcomes timber&rsquo;s natural weakness across the grain: the result is a panel that is strong and stiff in both directions, resists splitting and is dimensionally stable. It is used for structural flooring, concrete formwork and furniture carcasses. MDF (medium density fibreboard) is made from fine wood fibres bonded with resin under heat and pressure. It has no grain direction, machines cleanly and takes paint well, but it is not moisture-resistant and swells if wetted. OSB (oriented strand board) uses strands of wood bonded at specific angles for high strength and is used for structural wall sheathing. Ceramics (including engineering ceramics such as aluminium oxide and silicon carbide) are extremely hard and wear-resistant but brittle, so they are used for cutting tool inserts and heat-resistant linings &mdash; not structural applications subject to impact.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Structural Concrete as a Composite</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n13">Structural concrete is a composite of cement, aggregate (sand and gravel) and water. Concrete is very strong in compression but weak in tension. Reinforced concrete adds steel rebar in the tension zones: the concrete resists compression, the steel resists tension, and together they can carry bending loads that neither could handle alone. Prestressed concrete goes further: the steel is placed under tension before the concrete is cast, putting the concrete into permanent pre-compression so that it can resist higher tensile loads before cracking. HS2 tunnels and multi-storey car parks are typical applications.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n15">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n16">Thermoplastics soften on heating and can be reshaped repeatedly (ABS, acrylic, nylon, polycarbonate, polystyrene); thermosets form permanent cross-links on curing and cannot be remelted (epoxy, polyester resin, melamine, vulcanised rubber).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n17">Composites combine a matrix (usually polymer resin) with reinforcing fibres; properties depend on fibre type, direction, volume fraction and matrix &mdash; fibres must be aligned with the load direction for maximum effect.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18">Structural board products (plywood, MDF, OSB) and concrete are also composites; cross-graining in plywood and steel reinforcement in concrete overcome the directional weaknesses of timber and plain concrete respectively.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n14">A common exam mistake is writing that &lsquo;the fibres reinforce the composite&rsquo; without explaining the mechanism &mdash; this just repeats the question. Instead, explain that the fibres carry tensile load because they have high tensile strength and stiffness, while the matrix transfers the load between fibres and protects them from damage. Also, when answering questions about thermoplastics vs thermosets, use the keyword &lsquo;cross-links&rsquo; for thermosets &mdash; it shows you understand the molecular reason for the difference in behaviour, which extended-answer mark schemes reward.</p>

