  "marks": "Mastering (7-8): Addresses at least three distinct maintenance concerns with specific engineering reasons. For example: (1) Corrosion of steel beams from de-icing salt — must specify stainless steel fasteners, protective paint system, regular inspection and re-painting schedule; neglecting this is the most common cause of bridge structural degradation. (2) Concrete carbonation and chloride ingress — de-icing salt allows chloride ions to penetrate, causing rebar to rust and spall the concrete cover; design must specify minimum cover depth and regular chloride-content testing. (3) Wear of road surface and expansion joints — joints allow thermal expansion but collect grit that causes accelerated wear; service plan must include joint inspection and clearing. Response evaluates the cost of maintaining correctly against the cost of premature replacement or failure, and reaches a justified conclusion about the importance of designing maintenance in from the outset.\nSecure (5-6): Three concerns identified with reasonable engineering reasoning; evaluation present.\nDeveloping (3-4): Two concerns covered; reasoning may be partial or generic.\nEmerging (1-2): One concern named; no engineering justification."
 },
 {
  "text": "Helion Power Ltd is comparing two sites for a new energy generation facility: Site A is suitable for a tidal barrage, Site B is suitable for a biomass power station.\n\nDiscuss the environmental impact of each option on the local ecosystem and the wider environment. In your answer, use specific engineering and environmental mechanisms rather than general statements.",
  "type": "8 marks — Extended Response: Discuss Impact on Society and Environment",
  "marks": "Mastering (7-8): Tidal barrage: predictable power output tied to tidal cycle (not 24/7); no operational greenhouse emissions; but the barrage changes water flow and sediment transport in the estuary, potentially destroying intertidal habitats (mudflats, salt marsh) used by migratory birds; affects fish migration routes. Wider environment benefit: displaces fossil-fuel generation and associated CO2. Biomass: burns organic material releasing CO2, but if biomass is sustainably managed (regrown at the same rate as burned) the net CO2 is approximately zero over the growth cycle; transport of biomass adds vehicle emissions; risk of land-use change if non-local biomass is imported (deforestation). Response identifies that neither option has zero impact and evaluates which impact type (local ecosystem vs global climate) is more significant, reaching a reasoned conclusion.\nSecure (5-6): Both options covered with at least two specific mechanisms each; some comparison made.\nDeveloping (3-4): Both options described but mechanisms vague; comparison limited.\nEmerging (1-2): One option described or both described only in generic terms."
 }
]

